@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+//! What the write path costs, and the one thing that decides it: how many payloads share a call.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.5-batch-writes`. The store has written one record per append since
+//! `lakelet/4.1-ingest-protocol`, which means one open and one write per record — and that is the
+//! cost this module is about. Not the bytes that end up on the device: the number of calls it took
+//! to put them there. A device flush costs milliseconds and `lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr` measured
+//! one, but a write syscall and the open in front of it are not free either, and the same bytes
+//! cost less when they are handed to the kernel in one piece.
+//!
+//! Two things live here, and the split is the step:
+//!
+//! * [`Batcher`] is the payloads that arrived together and the one call that writes them once the
+//!   caller says the turn is over. It is what makes "these frames were one batch" a value a caller
+//!   can hold, and it is how a daemon keeps the promise [`Spool::append`] has always made: nothing
+//!   is answered before it is written, and a group is written before *any* of it is answered;
+//! * [`Stats`] is what the write path has cost, counted — records, bytes, calls (the opens
+//!   included), device flushes and batches. Those are the numbers this step's page and the course's
+//!   budget are written in, so that "it got faster" is a claim somebody can check instead of an
+//!   impression.
+//!
+//! What is deliberately **not** here: the bytes one record is made of
+//! ([`crate::engine::wal::segment`]), the decision about waiting for the device
+//! ([`crate::engine::wal::Policy`]) and the log itself — [`crate::engine::spool`] owns that, and a
+//! batch reaches it through one call.
+//!
+//! [`Spool::append`]: crate::engine::Spool::append
+
+pub mod batcher;
+
+pub use batcher::{Batcher, Stats};
